# The RAW sources only. They exist so corpus-verify-provenance and src/check_extraction.py
# can check the documents against what they came from, and both run in CI, never in the
# server. ~9 MB of the repo.
_meta/snapshots/*.pdf
_meta/snapshots/*.xml

# THE .txt EXTRACTIONS STAY -- they are a RUNTIME dependency, which is not obvious.
# src/citation_schemes.py reads _meta/snapshots/2-cfr-200-2021-02-21.txt AT IMPORT to build
# the set of sections that existed before the 2021-02-22 consolidation; that is what lets
# `2 CFR 200.56` answer "removed on 2021-02-22" instead of "no such section". Excluding the
# whole directory was tried first and the framework raised FileNotFoundError before serving
# a single request.

# Build/verification-only, never imported by the server.
.github/
_meta/.cache/

# NOT .git — IT IS A RUNTIME DEPENDENCY. The FTS cache key is `git rev-parse HEAD` plus a
# hash of `git status --porcelain`, and corpus_overview() shells out to `git log -1`. Drop it
# and repo_state() collapses to a constant, so content changes are never picked up — silently,
# which is the worst way for a corpus to be wrong. The Dockerfile header says the same thing;
# it is repeated here because this is the file where someone would try to remove it.

# Mounted at runtime from the host, not copied. The artifact is built out of band on a GPU
# machine; a git-clone build cannot see it anyway (gitignored), and COPYing it would put
# hundreds of MB of vectors into an image that reads them from a bind mount.
_meta/embeddings/
